# Git, PR, and code quality (all repos)

## Git safety

- Never update git config.
- **Single checkout only — no git worktrees.** Do not run `git worktree add` or create parallel repo folders (e.g. `X_booking_dev01_*`). One repo directory, one branch checked out. Remove stray worktrees with `git worktree remove` when found.
- **Before any install, build, scrape, edit, or verification:** confirm `git branch --show-current` matches the task and `git switch <branch>` first. Never assume branch from another folder or an earlier step.
- No destructive git (`push --force`, `reset --hard`, `clean`) unless user explicitly requests.
- No `--no-verify` unless user explicitly requests.
- Never force-push to `main`/`master`; warn if requested.
- Avoid `git commit --amend` unless user requested, last commit was yours this session, and not pushed.
- Hook failure → fix and **new** commit (never amend failed commit).
- No secrets in commits (`.env`, credentials, API keys).
- Do not commit unless the user asks.

## Pull requests

- Use `gh` when user asks for PR tasks.
- Before PR: `git status`, diff, log, full branch history vs base.
- Push with `-u` only when needed; do not push unless user asks.

## Code quality

- Readability and correctness over cleverness.
- Complete syntactically valid files (no placeholder TODOs unless user asked for partial diff).
- Preserve project structure and public interfaces unless refactor required.
- Remove dead code and unused imports when refactoring.
- Explicit names; avoid `helper.py`, `misc.py`, `temp.py`.
- Add/update tests when changing business logic, DB logic, or critical workflows.

## Python

- Target 3.11+ unless project specifies otherwise.
- Type hints on public and important internal functions.
- Prefer `pathlib.Path`, context managers, specific exceptions, f-strings (not for SQL).
- `if __name__ == "__main__":` for script entrypoints.

## Configuration and secrets

- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, tokens, credentials.
- Env vars for secrets and runtime config; `.env` local only; provide `.env.example` for new vars.
- Validate required env vars at startup; fail fast.
- Never log secrets, passwords, tokens, or full database URLs.

## MySQL (when applicable)

- `mysql-connector-python` unless project standardizes otherwise.
- Parameterized queries only; never interpolate user input into SQL.
- Env vars: `MYSQL_HOST`, `MYSQL_PORT`, `MYSQL_USER`, `MYSQL_PASSWORD`, `MYSQL_DATABASE`.
- Transactions for writes; roll back on failure.

## Dependencies

- Add only necessary dependencies; prefer stdlib when adequate.
- Update `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml` consistently.
- Verify on [PyPI](https://pypi.org/) before adding.

## Research / clinical boundaries

- **ORSI, AR_pipeline, campania_transplant:** research/training/HEOR artifacts — not clinical decision support or robot control.
- **PS_robot_adaptor:** mock/sim/replay/visualization only; no real robot control.
- **kidney_federico:** clinical data; never commit PHI.

## Communication

- Clear, complete sentences; proportional length.
- Summarize what changed and list affected files.
- Markdown links for URLs/paths when helpful.
- End with validation steps when relevant; no filler follow-ups.

## Absolute prohibitions

- No hardcoded secrets.
- No SQL string interpolation for user data.
- No silent exception swallowing.
- No destructive CLI/git without explicit approval.
- No committing or pushing without explicit approval.
